.TH LOCAL 3
.SH NAME
local \- folders of the computer the browser runs on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -c #L /mnt/term
.BI "bind -c #L" name " dir

.B /mnt/term/ctl
.BI /mnt/term/ name /...

.B local
.B local add
.RI [ dir ]
.B local allow
.RI [ name ...]
.B local remove
.IR name ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On p9web,
.B #L
holds folders of the computer that runs the browser, each one that the user has chosen.
The browser's File System Access API serves them; Chrome has it, Safari does not.
.PP
Each folder is a directory in
.BR #L ,
by the folder's name, and
.BI #L name
is that folder alone, to
.I bind
anywhere:
.EX
	bind -c '#Lsrc' $home/src
.EE
A line like that in a profile binds the folder at each start.
.PP
The browser keeps the folders that the user chose, and the permission to use them, across
visits.
After a reload, Chrome asks once again for that permission: the page shows a bar with
.BR Allow ,
whose prompt can say
.IR "Allow on every visit" ,
and then it does not ask again.
Until the user allows the folder, a walk into it fails, and the bar shows.
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
gives a line for each folder: its permission,
.BR granted ,
.BR prompt
or
.BR denied ,
and its name.
It takes these messages, each with at most one name, the rest of the line:
.TP
.B add
The page asks the user for a folder with the browser's chooser.
The write returns when the user has chosen one, or has not.
A read of the same open
.B ctl
then gives the folder's name.
.TP
.BR allow " [\fIname\fP]"
The page asks the user to allow the folder, or all the folders that have no permission.
.TP
.BI remove " name"
The folder leaves
.BR #L .
The files in it stay as they are.
.PP
.I Local
runs these:
with no arguments it prints
.BR ctl ;
.B local add
.I dir
binds the new folder on
.IR dir ,
which it makes if it must.
.PP
The browser chooser needs a click or a key in the page.
The newline that runs
.B local add
is one, if the chooser opens soon after it; otherwise the page shows a bar with a button to
choose the folder.
.PP
.B #L
holds a file open for writing in memory, and writes it to the folder half a second after the
last write, at its last close, and when the system shuts down; a reload loses at most that
half second.
A read while it is open sees what the writes put there.
If the browser cannot write it, the console says so.
The folder has no modes or owners: a directory is 775, a file 775, and both are the host
owner's, who alone may write, create, remove and use
.BR ctl .
A rename is the browser's
.IR move ;
to rename or remove a folder itself, use the computer's own tools.
.SH SOURCE
.B k/src/kernel/localfs.ts
.br
.B k/src/main/localui.ts
.br
.B rc/bin/local
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR namespace (4)
.SH BUGS
Chrome alone has the chooser.
.B #L
cannot write a file larger than memory.
A file open for writing does not see what other programs on the computer change in it until
it is next opened.
